WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.033 --> 00:00:02.033
-Welcome back to "Late Night."

00:00:02.033 --> 00:00:03.434
We're here
with Allison Williams.

00:00:03.434 --> 00:00:05.067
Arlo, I do want to ask before
we talk about the podcast.

00:00:05.067 --> 00:00:06.667
Is he a performer as well?

00:00:06.667 --> 00:00:09.534
Do you sense that,
having performers as parents?

00:00:09.534 --> 00:00:11.634
-He's a little bit
of a performer in that

00:00:11.634 --> 00:00:13.067
he will sing occasionally.

00:00:13.067 --> 00:00:15.100
He has a little guitar
that he likes to strum,

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:21.033
but he's very bossy about the
way I am during his performance.

00:00:21.033 --> 00:00:23.801
-Interesting.
-Which I'm working very hard

00:00:23.801 --> 00:00:26.968
to not allow hurt my feelings.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:26.968 --> 00:00:28.634
-What does he need more of?
Yeah.

00:00:28.634 --> 00:00:31.133
-Well, he needs less of me,
just in general.

00:00:31.133 --> 00:00:32.767
[ Laughter ]

00:00:32.767 --> 00:00:34.200
But kind of specifically,

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:35.667
so sometimes I'm allowed
to sing with him

00:00:35.667 --> 00:00:36.868
and then other people aren't.

00:00:36.868 --> 00:00:38.367
Sometimes I'm excluded
from singing from him,

00:00:38.367 --> 00:00:41.033
but other people can't.
Sometimes I can't dance.

00:00:41.033 --> 00:00:44.000
Sometimes I'm allowed
to just dance and no singing.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:46.167
Sometimes I have to stay
perfectly still.

00:00:46.167 --> 00:00:47.868
Sometimes I'm not allowed
to look at him.

00:00:47.868 --> 00:00:49.868
-Okay.
-One might say I'm giving him

00:00:49.868 --> 00:00:51.701
too much power.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:51.701 --> 00:00:53.901
And that is why he will be
in therapy later.

00:00:53.901 --> 00:00:56.868
I owe him a reason
for therapy later in life.

00:00:56.868 --> 00:00:58.868
-Does he have a favorite song?

00:00:58.868 --> 00:01:00.400
Does he have a go-to?

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:02.667
-His favorite song is "Lover"
by Taylor Swift.

00:01:02.667 --> 00:01:05.734
-Well, a lot of...
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:05.734 --> 00:01:09.100
-It's a really -- I can't say
I wasn't involved in that.

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:10.968
-They say it's a perfect song
for 2-year-olds.

00:01:10.968 --> 00:01:13.601
-It's a perfect song for
a 2-year-old and his mom.

00:01:13.601 --> 00:01:15.234
Another reason
to send him to therapy.

00:01:15.234 --> 00:01:19.467
-It's like, "And then she'd make
me do 'Lover' and...

00:01:19.467 --> 00:01:22.434
And she wouldn't look at me."
[ Laughter ]

00:01:22.434 --> 00:01:24.067
Alright, so this podcast,
this is fascinating.

00:01:24.067 --> 00:01:25.501
This is a true-crime podcast.

00:01:25.501 --> 00:01:29.300
This is not a story I knew of,
but a lot of names in this,

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:31.634
in this story that I think
we're all aware of.

00:01:31.634 --> 00:01:34.133
Can you tell us a little bit
about Elma Sands and the story?

00:01:34.133 --> 00:01:37.567
-So basically the way
I was introduced to it

00:01:37.567 --> 00:01:40.868
is a woman named Allison Flom
who created and directed

00:01:40.868 --> 00:01:42.367
and wrote the whole thing,

00:01:42.367 --> 00:01:44.033
said I was a tour guide
in Manhattan,

00:01:44.033 --> 00:01:45.367
and I discovered this well,

00:01:45.367 --> 00:01:47.734
in the basement of a store
in SoHo that was unmarked,

00:01:47.734 --> 00:01:50.534
and I learned that it was
the site of a murder in 1799

00:01:50.534 --> 00:01:53.000
during a pandemic.
And in that murder trial,

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:56.634
which was the first murder trial
with the new U.S. Constitution,

00:01:56.634 --> 00:01:59.367
the two lawyers who were
co-counsel for the defense,

00:01:59.367 --> 00:02:01.767
her murderer,
were Hamilton and Burr.

00:02:01.767 --> 00:02:03.400
And so there's like -- A gasp.

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:04.968
[ Laughter ]

00:02:04.968 --> 00:02:07.767
So there was -- there's a line
about it really quickly

00:02:07.767 --> 00:02:09.868
in "Hamilton" where they say,
"Levi Weeks is innocent.

00:02:09.868 --> 00:02:12.067
Call your first witness.
That's all you had to say."

00:02:12.067 --> 00:02:14.934
And he is the person
that they're defending.

00:02:14.934 --> 00:02:17.033
But there's no mention
of Elma in there.

00:02:17.033 --> 00:02:19.767
And so this goes back
through very flawed

00:02:19.767 --> 00:02:23.701
court transcripts from the time
trying to reconstruct the trial

00:02:23.701 --> 00:02:25.767
with a kind of radio play,

00:02:25.767 --> 00:02:28.968
true crime with modern language.

00:02:28.968 --> 00:02:30.234
So it's a little bit
more accessible,

00:02:30.234 --> 00:02:31.901
but it's just like
full of details

00:02:31.901 --> 00:02:33.634
that are too crazy
to be believed,

00:02:33.634 --> 00:02:36.300
like it was the first time
anyone used a stenographer.

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:40.467
And this stenographer was the
tutor of Philip, Hamilton's son.

00:02:40.467 --> 00:02:43.534
So we're guessing
it's not, like, super accurate.

00:02:43.534 --> 00:02:45.934
It has a lot of descriptions
of the prosecutor,

00:02:45.934 --> 00:02:49.367
like being exhausted
and like haggard

00:02:49.367 --> 00:02:50.667
and like Hamilton and Burr,

00:02:50.667 --> 00:02:53.234
just these, like,
shining heroes of the case.

00:02:53.234 --> 00:02:54.868
But it's really interesting
to think about,

00:02:54.868 --> 00:02:57.734
like, this is the first trial
with a brand-new Constitution.

00:02:57.734 --> 00:02:59.968
They could have done
some like wild, creative things,

00:02:59.968 --> 00:03:02.567
and they just baked
all of the old systems

00:03:02.567 --> 00:03:04.033
right into it
from the beginning.

00:03:04.033 --> 00:03:07.367
It's just interesting
to delve back into that world

00:03:07.367 --> 00:03:08.634
and then walk around New York

00:03:08.634 --> 00:03:10.200
and realize that you're still
in that world.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:12.167
SoHo was this area
called the Lispenard,

00:03:12.167 --> 00:03:14.901
and there was the Manhattan well
in the middle of this meadow.

00:03:14.901 --> 00:03:16.834
And it's just so hard
to imagine,

00:03:16.834 --> 00:03:18.734
and it's so close
at the same time.

00:03:18.734 --> 00:03:20.100
-Oh, it's a really cool project,

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:21.934
and it's always just
the best to see you.

00:03:21.934 --> 00:03:23.534
Thank you so much
for coming back, Allison.

00:03:23.534 --> 00:03:24.567
-Aw, thank you for having me.

00:03:24.567 --> 00:03:25.901
-You guys, that's
Allison Williams.

00:03:25.901 --> 00:03:29.367
"Erased: The Murder of
Elma Sands" is available

00:03:29.367 --> 00:03:31.234
on Apple Podcasts now.
It's great.

00:03:31.234 --> 00:03:32.801
-We'll be right back
with Fisher Stevens.

